"Shows fitted-model residuals in observation order to reveal drift, runs, cycles, and unmodeled serial structure.\n\nUse when: Observation order carries meaningful temporal or production information. A model assumes independent errors or stable residual behavior.\n\nAvoid when: Rows were arbitrarily sorted or the actual observation sequence is unknown.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes acquisition order or elapsed time. Vertical position encodes a named residual, with zero as the reference.\n\nBuild: Recover the original acquisition sequence after fitting. Compute residuals using the model’s correct definition and inclusion rules. Draw ordered points or a thin connecting path and annotate known process boundaries.\n\nCheck: Check ordering was not replaced by fitted-value or response sorting. Verify missing observation intervals are visible when time is used. Confirm residual units or standardization are labeled.\n\nPitfalls: Connecting residuals is a diagnostic aid and does not create extra observations. A flat residual sequence does not prove independence; inspect autocorrelation and lag plots as complementary checks.\n\nExample: Review a synthetic sensor calibration model by plotting residuals in collection order and annotating a documented instrument change.\n\nAlternatives: Autocorrelation plot, Lag plot, Residual plot."
